## Symptom

`karr move` hangs indefinitely and grows without bound until the machine is out
of memory. Observed in `~/dev/perl/io-k8s-p5`:

```
$ karr move 1 in-progress
# ... never returns
```

After ~4 minutes the process held **53 GB RSS (80% of a 62 GB machine)** and was
still growing. It has to be killed from outside; it never fails on its own.

Re-run under `ulimit -v 4000000` to cap it, the actual error surfaces:

```
Use of uninitialized value in hash element at FFI/CheckLib.pm line 259 during global destruction.
Use of uninitialized value in hash element at FFI/CheckLib.pm line 259 during global destruction.
Use of uninitialized value in hash element at FFI/CheckLib.pm line 259 during global destruction.
Deep recursion on subroutine "FFI::Platypus::TypeParser::Version1::parse"
    at FFI/Platypus/TypeParser/Version1.pm line 207 during global destruction.
Out of memory!
```

`Git::Libgit2` (0.004) is the FFI::Platypus consumer in the dependency chain, so
that is where the recursion lives.

## Impact

The board cannot be mutated at all in the affected repository — no `move`, so no
status tracking. Worse than a plain failure: an agent that calls `karr move`
unattended will eat all system memory until something else on the machine gets
OOM-killed. There is no timeout and no self-limiting behaviour.

## What is ruled out

Each of these was tested against a throwaway board (`git init` + `karr create`)
and **works correctly**:

| hypothesis | test | result |
|---|---|---|
| `move` is broken generally | `karr move 1 in-progress` in a fresh repo | clean error: `Status 'in-progress' requires --claim` |
| the validation/error path itself | same as above | clean, exits normally |
| board is missing `refs/karr/config` | both boards only have `meta/next-id` + `tasks/*/data`; neither has a config ref | not the difference |
| large / non-ASCII ticket body | created a ticket with the exact 5 KB UTF-8 body of the affected ticket, then moved it | `Moved task 2: backlog -> todo`, clean |
| source status `todo` (not `backlog`) | `karr move 2 in-progress` from `todo` in the fresh repo | clean error, exits normally |

Read paths are unaffected: `karr list`, `karr show` and `karr create` all work
normally in the affected repository.

Note the failing invocation was `karr move 1 in-progress` **without** `--claim` —
i.e. an invocation that should have died immediately on validation with
`Status 'in-progress' requires --claim`, as it does in the fresh repo. So the
runaway happens *before* that check, while loading, not while writing.

## Remaining suspect

The one variable not yet isolated is the repository itself. `io-k8s-p5` is a
large repo (~750 modules, long history); the throwaway board was a single empty
commit. A probe ticket created directly in the affected board would separate
"this repository" from "this one ticket" — I did not run it to avoid leaving
scratch tickets on a live board.

## Suggested direction

Two separate concerns, both worth fixing independently of the root cause:

1. **The recursion itself** — likely a `Git::Libgit2` / FFI::Platypus type
   parser cycle triggered during global destruction. The `FFI::CheckLib`
   uninitialized-value warnings on the same line suggest teardown ordering:
   something is still resolving FFI types after the library handle is gone.
2. **The unbounded growth** — regardless of the trigger, `karr` should not be
   able to consume all system memory. A runaway in a git helper should fail
   loudly, not take the machine with it. Worth a guard, given karr is explicitly
   built to be driven by unattended agents.
Reproduced with Git::Libgit2 0.005 (ticket said 0.004) — the version bump does NOT fix it. Also reproduced with a purely LOCAL file origin, so SSH/network is not involved.

ISOLATION DONE. Repo size/history is a red herring; io-k8s-p5 is only 7.3M with 2 karr refs. The real variable is:

  fresh 'git init' + one karr ticket + NO remote      -> clean, prints "Status 'in-progress' requires --claim", exits 0
  fresh 'git init' + one karr ticket + a local remote -> same message, THEN unbounded FFI recursion during global destruction

Correction to the ticket: the runaway does NOT happen before the --claim check. The check runs and prints correctly; the process then hangs in GLOBAL DESTRUCTION. Earlier runs just lost the message in the warning flood.

Mechanism: move calls sync_before, which stores an App::karr::SyncGuard on $self->_sync_guard. The croak from validation unwinds, but the command object is kept alive (MooX::Cmd command_chain cycles), so the guard is only reaped during global destruction. SyncGuard::DESTROY then calls $git->push, which does fresh FFI work (Git::Native remote + credential closure). With no remote, push short-circuits at 'return 1 unless $repo->has_remote' and no FFI work happens — which is exactly why the ticket's throwaway board looked clean.
## Narrowed further: write paths are fine, it is `move` specifically

In the same affected repository (io-k8s-p5), on the same board, these all work
normally and exit cleanly:

- `karr create "..." --priority high --tags a,b --body "<5 KB body>"` (twice)
- `karr edit <id> --body "<3 KB body>"`
- `karr list`, `karr show`

Only `karr move` runs away. So it is not "writing to a large repository" and not
libgit2 write access in general -- it is something on the move path in
particular, before the `--claim` validation is reached. That validation is the
part that works fine on a fresh board, which makes the loading/preparation step
that `move` does before it the prime suspect.
ROOT CAUSE (confirmed, reproduced both directions).

Chain: move -> sync_before -> guard stashed on the command object -> validation croaks -> bin/karr's handler prints the error and exits -> the guard is never released, so it is first reaped during GLOBAL DESTRUCTION -> SyncGuard::DESTROY runs a full $git->push -> Git::Native/libgit2 through FFI::Platypus. By that phase Perl has already freed FFI::CheckLib's $pattern/@path and FFI::Platypus's type-parser globals, so Git::Libgit2::FFI::ffi() rebuilds from scratch, find_lib re-runs against undefined globals (the 'uninitialized value' warnings) and TypeParser::Version1::parse recurses without bound. Measured growth: 1 GB in 1.5s, ~700 MB/s.

So the FFI recursion is real but it is a SYMPTOM of karr calling into libgit2 at a phase where libgit2 must not be called. Nothing needs fixing in Git::Libgit2.

Also worth recording: the insurance push has never actually worked on the CLI die path. bin/karr catches the die and exits, so the guard ALWAYS reached global destruction, where the push either recursed to OOM or died as '(in cleanup) Can't call method push on an undefined value' depending on teardown order. It was never a timely push.

FIX (2 layers, no new deps):
 * lib/App/karr/Git.pm - _repo and is_repo refuse every native op while ${^GLOBAL_PHASE} eq 'DESTRUCT', setting last_error. This is the hard backstop for concern 2: it caps the whole class of bug, not just this call site, because every native op funnels through _repo and every caller already handles a false _repo.
 * lib/App/karr/SyncGuard.pm - DESTROY reports instead of pushing in that phase.
 * Git.pm gains pending_writes (counts write_ref/delete_ref) so the guard stays quiet when the body died before writing anything, and only warns when local refs really are unpushed. Without it every 'forgot --claim' would print misleading 'run karr sync' advice.

