FLAKY SUBTEST FIXED (coordinator report). Diagnosis confirmed, not adopted on faith.

Instrumented SyncGuard::DESTROY and ran the exact subtest-1 scenario 60x:
  52 x  git=DEF   pending_writes=0  -> silent (correct)
   8 x  git=UNDEF unreadable        -> warned (spurious)
The write count was 0 in every readable run; the ONLY variable was whether the blessed $git had already been reaped. So ~13% of plain usage errors printed sync advice for a board nothing had written to. Coordinator's diagnosis was exactly right.

Why it happens: perl tears down global destruction in two passes — sv_clean_objs() destroys every blessed object first (that is where DESTROY runs), then sv_clean_all() frees the rest. Any blessed object is therefore a coin flip inside DESTROY; a plain non-object SV is not.

FIX: the counter moved from per-object $self->{_writes} to the package scalar $App::karr::Git::WRITES, and SyncGuard's DESTRUCT branch now reads that scalar and touches no object at all. Re-instrumented, 60 runs each:
  unwritten board: WRITES=0 in 60/60 (10 of them with git already UNDEF), 0 'Push skipped'
  written board:   WRITES=1 in 60/60 (52 of them with git already UNDEF), 60 'Push skipped'
Both directions now decided by real state instead of teardown order.

